   "category": "tax"
  },
  "summary": "ITAT clarifies that property sellers receiving payment in staggered instalments must pay full capital gains tax in Year 1 upon agreement execution. Hook: 'Selling a flat on instalments? The tax department demands full capital gains tax upfront.'",
  "notes": "- ITAT Pune decision clarifying capital gains liability under Section 45(1) read with Section 53A of Transfer of Property Act\n- Tax liability arises in the financial year when the agreement to sell and possession/rights are handed over, not when instalments are actually credited\n- Major cash flow trap: sellers receiving staggered payments over 2-3 years must still pay 100% of capital gains tax in Year 1\n- Failure to pay upfront attracts interest under Section 234B/234C and penalty notices from the Income Tax department\n- Structuring protection: linking possession handover strictly to final tranche or using conditional sale deeds",
  "actions": [
   "Created video idea 2026-11-08-property-instalment-tax-trap"
  ],
  "analyzed_at": "2026-11-08T04:02:30Z"
 },
